26
00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:16.200
The point is the richness and wonder
of the world beyond its borders.

27
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.240
I come from the Nam
June Paik school of using technology

28
00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:24.880
to despise it properly, and we have enough
celebrations of technology.

29
00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:26.920
It is a very new project as well.

30
00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:28.760
It's essentially didn't exist

31
00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:32.520
until about March this year,
so it's evolved in a breakneck pace.

32
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.320
The heart of this project
is a consideration of environmental

33
00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:40.720
sensing in the guise of the much heralded
new AI technology.

34
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:43.040
So language, vision, models, etc.

35
00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:44.400
and thinking about

36
00:01:44.400 --> 00:01:48.160
what are their implications
for seeing and thinking going forward?

37
00:01:48.200 --> 00:01:52.160
The digital world, 
no question, has an extremely difficult

38
00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.800
relationship with the environment
in terms of the energy that's used,

39
00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:00.720
the waste that's generated at every stage
of the lifecycle of digital technologies.

40
00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.640
And that bothers me as an artist who uses
digital technologies as their medium.

41
00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:08.160
That's something
I am not particularly happy about.

42
00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:12.320
But that said, I think it's worth
noting that although contemporary

43
00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.800
AI technologies have been critiqued
for their environmental impact,

44
00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:17.280
things were bad before.

45
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.520
It wasn't as if things were great
even before these technologies kind of

46
00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:23.560
came onto the scene,
and it was certainly not looking good.

47
00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:27.360
We've got these systems now,
and certainly in the very sort of

48
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:30.800
generalized, mythologized sense
in which they're pitched towards us.

49
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:34.000
And earlier iterations of technology
marketed as AI,

50
00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:37.280
a common refrain is that they will
somehow save the planet.

51
00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:39.680
They're advanced analytical techniques.

52
00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.520
That number crunching capability
was somehow going to sort everything out,

53
00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.040
and we don't have to worry
too much about their impacts as a result.

54
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:50.880
And that has been fairly robustly
critiqued as a load of greenwashing.

55
00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.600
Then that's before
we even get into thinking

56
00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.600
quite cogently about the technology,
what it can actually measure,

57
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.600
map and model about the world
realistically.

58
00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:04.480
Nevertheless,
we are here, and it is very likely,

59
00:03:04.480 --> 00:03:08.760
I think, that this technology
in its current form, will persist

60
00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.920
in some form of future and will be
part of the public political tool set

61
00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:16.880
through which environmental issues
are seen and understood by many people.

62
00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:18.960
People are going to query ChatGPT.

63
00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:21.480
They are not going to read
the scientific paper.

64
00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.320
So if we take that premise, then

65
00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.440
what other possibilities do exist
for these technologies?

66
00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:29.200
Can we use them fruitfully?

67
00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.920
Can we use them thoughtfully?

68
00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:34.720
Can we use them with respect to what
they actually are, rather than what

69
00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:36.400
then sort of typically sold to us?

70
00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:40.960
As Sabina Linnell in this vein has called
for the development

71
00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:45.520
of environmental intelligence
AI that is not oriented towards

72
00:03:45.520 --> 00:03:49.760
emulating human modes of thought,
human imperatives, human agency,

73
00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:54.360
but actually drawing our attention to the
more than human world or non-human world.

74
00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.480
As she frames it,
it scales its durations, its imperatives,

75
00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:00.920
and how the impact our daily lives.

76
00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:05.200
And it's here, then,
that my project appears in, very much

77
00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:06.440
inspired by this call.

78
00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:10.280
But coming out of prior work
that I've done, thinking about how we can

79
00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:14.200
use drones and machine vision
as environmental platforms, I'm

80
00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:15.480
doing something similar here,

81
00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.400
but instead I'm trying to create
an AI driven sensor platform.

82
00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:23.480
Very, very small compact package
that is small enough, light enough,

83
00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:24.760
and low power enough.

84
00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:28.520
You can carry it aboard a kite
rather than a drone, as we might

85
00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:29.680
typically expect.

86
00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:33.320
And I think this image actually is one
I really like taking AI outside

87
00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:36.720
of its sprawling,
all consuming data center,

88
00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:41.120
ripping it out there, shrinking it down,
making it small, making it vulnerable,

89
00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:42.600
putting it back in its box.

90
00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:46.160
Frankly, a box
you can hold and throwing it up there.

91
00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.600
Let's see. No,
enough of these cloud computing metaphors.

92
00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:49.920
Let's make that happen.

93
00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:51.880
Let's make AI small again.

94
00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:53.680
Let's make it vulnerable again.

95
00:04:53.680 --> 00:04:57.000
Let's make it something that respects
its footprint in the world.

96
00:04:58.280 --> 00:04:58.880
There is

97
00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:02.920
a awful lot that I could say
about the technology of this program,

98
00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:06.280
what I'm doing with it, and
I just don't have the time to go into it.

99
00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:08.040
So I'm going to give a very rapid sketch.

100
00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:12.880
Now, essentially,
it consists of a large lifting kite.

101
00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:15.800
In this case
it was a big eight foot Delta Conine.

102
00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:20.080
And below it is a sense of boom that's got
aerodynamic plates on either end

103
00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:24.120
