that boom in the winds aloft.

104
00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:28.640
Attached to that is a small computing
package, typically a Raspberry Pi.

105
00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:31.800
And then attached to that package
is all manner of different sensors.

106
00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:36.080
I've primarily been working with images
because that's what I understand best.

107
00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:38.680
But there will be more sense
as they are attached to it.

108
00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:40.280
Hopefully very, very soon.

109
00:05:40.280 --> 00:05:42.400
But in essence, once we've got all that,

110
00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.720
what the Raspberry Pi is doing
is it's taking in that data,

111
00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:49.920
feeding it into a small, large vision
model

112
00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:53.680
and setting various tasks
and analysis on it.

113
00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:57.960
The models I've been using are Alibaba,
clouds, Quinn and Google's Gemma.

114
00:05:57.960 --> 00:06:00.000
2 to 4 billion parameters.

115
00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.000
So these are really quite small models,
very much edge case instances.

116
00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:07.040
And frankly pulling them to work,
seeing what they can do.

117
00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:10.400
The whole lot is controlled
in a manner typical of a drone actually.

118
00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:13.560
So there's a
there's a kite net, you log into the kite,

119
00:06:13.560 --> 00:06:17.840
it has its own Wi-Fi address,
and that allows for commands to be down,

120
00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:21.840
commands to be up
linked video, imagery and data to be down,

121
00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:23.280
linked in real time as well.

122
00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:26.280
So again very much like a typical drone.

123
00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:29.480
So okay,
we've got this very elaborate system.

124
00:06:29.480 --> 00:06:31.440
Then we've gone to all the trouble
of hauling it up.

125
00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:32.480
What does it actually do.

126
00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:33.880
What am I trying to get it to do?

127
00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:35.840
So in terms of visual data,

128
00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:39.840
I'm simply trying to get the thing
to classify the scene sensibly, to

129
00:06:39.880 --> 00:06:44.040
go, okay, I have this scene before me
at an altitude of 200ft.

130
00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:45.080
What am I looking at?

131
00:06:45.080 --> 00:06:47.440
What are the natural features
in that scene?

132
00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:50.800
And particularly
using a land class sort of

133
00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:54.320
star filters, which are kind of typically
for what we use for satellites.

134
00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:57.560
I'm bringing it down here and saying,
okay, classify

135
00:06:57.560 --> 00:07:00.880
this terrain according
to these different land class types.

136
00:07:00.960 --> 00:07:02.560
It turns out that's incredibly hard.

137
00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.040
Language vision
models are trained on people and things,

138
00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:07.040
and it only gets worse
the smaller they get.

139
00:07:07.040 --> 00:07:08.560
And so essentially, it's

140
00:07:08.560 --> 00:07:11.600
very difficult to get it to pay attention
to the natural world.

141
00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:13.760
The natural world is an interesting
backdrop.

142
00:07:13.760 --> 00:07:15.640
Surely you're interested in cars.

143
00:07:15.640 --> 00:07:17.480
Surely you're interested in people.

144
00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:20.080
Surely you are interested in buildings.
Well, the answer is no.

145
00:07:20.080 --> 00:07:20.720
No I'm not.

146
00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:24.080
But it's very hard
to get it to steer away from that.

147
00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:28.160
And even when it does point out a natural
scene, it's like there was a bush.

148
00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:30.960
There's a tree over there somewhere.
Well that's fantastic.

149
00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:32.520
What do we learn from that? Very little.

150
00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:35.120
So I'm trying to wrestle
with this technology

151
00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:38.520
and ultimately its failings
and its paucity

152
00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:40.680
are so different from the rich works

153
00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:43.920
of environmentally literature
that we are used to think of J.R.

154
00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:46.000
Carpenter's works, for instance.

155
00:07:46.000 --> 00:07:48.000
And that work was mentioned yesterday.

156
00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:49.160
And of course, we had

157
00:07:49.160 --> 00:07:50.360
we had the kind of documenting

158
00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:53.360
the imperceptible yesterday
that was a lovely presentation

159
00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:55.800
and really got this idea
of more than human

160
00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:58.800
sensing in all its richness
and all its alien.

161
00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:01.640
These language
vision models are wholly incapable

162
00:08:01.640 --> 00:08:05.200
of doing anything remotely as clever
as that without a lot of work.

163
00:08:05.200 --> 00:08:06.960
But that's part of the point.

164
00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:09.960
You know, so many of us,
so many researchers,

165
00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:14.040
rush to get these exhausting technologies
into their work to see what they can do,

166
00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:15.480
and that's absolutely fine.

167
00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:18.440
But it's also good to have projects
which point out, you know what?

168
00:08:18.440 --> 00:08:19.200
They're rubbish.

169
00:08:19.200 --> 00:08:22.200
There are so many things they don't do,
they don't do well.

170
00:08:22.240 --> 00:08:23.600
They're not very effective

171
00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:26.800
at to highlight their failures,
to highlight their failings.

172
00:08:26.840 --> 00:08:31.080
And ultimately, by way of that,
getting us to remind ourselves of a world

173
00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:33.520
that is so much richer
than these technologies,

174
00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:37.560
so much more alien
in it's becoming more than human voices

175
00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:42.600
that are not reducible to straightforward
modalities of data that cannot be put

176
00:08:42.600 --> 00:08:47.160
inside the neat black boxes we otherwise
wish the world could be put into.

177
00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:49.360
That's part of the idea.

178
00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:53.280
But nevertheless, you might think, okay,
where's the angle going on in all of this?

179
00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:57.040
So ultimately, what I hope to do 
and what I have been doing,

180
00:08:57.040 --> 00:09:01.360
I should say, is once the system
classifies the landscape,

181
00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:06.160
those classes and those short descriptions
associated with each class in terms of

182
00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:12.040
what it's seeing are then used as lookups
and a vector database to extract material

183
00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:16.560
of kind of various kind of books
and articles like put inside the system.

184
00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:20.200
And they are all works by different
writers such as Jennifer Gabriel.

185
00:09:20.280 --> 00:09:25.080
You'll see Mia Bennett,
who are all thinking about the nature

186
00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:29.240
of environmental sensing and sense
making, what it can do, what it can't do,

187
00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:30.880
what its limitations are.

188
00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:35.600
So the idea is that
the system is actually using what it sees

189
00:09:35.640 --> 00:09:39.440
as a framing query,
and is overlaying the visual landscape

190
00:09:39.440 --> 00:09:41.360
