196
00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:04.080
So it sees a distant hill,
it sees a river, it sees a farmland.

197
00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:05.840
Well,
what are these authors written about?

198
00:10:05.840 --> 00:10:08.520
The intersection of farmland
and digital sensing.

199
00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:11.600
What is the relationship
between digital sensing and rivers?

200
00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:15.280
It's never quite a one on one
correspondence, but I rather like that.

201
00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:15.680
Actually.

202
00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:19.640
I like the strangeness of
some of the outputs that come out of that.

203
00:10:19.640 --> 00:10:22.640
Reading the landscape
as a prompt for reflection,

204
00:10:22.640 --> 00:10:26.440
rather than some immediate insights
and the kind of narrow sense of the word.

205
00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:30.080
So again, part of the idea is
the system is generating

206
00:10:30.120 --> 00:10:34.520
a meditation on the nature of digital
sensing and sense making what it does

207
00:10:34.520 --> 00:10:38.520
not see, what it does
not know, the things that it cannot do

208
00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:42.920
when it comes to characterizing human
and more than human relationships.

209
00:10:43.640 --> 00:10:46.000
So coming up to near the end, very good.

210
00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:47.560
So I'm going to be the first to admit

211
00:10:47.560 --> 00:10:51.360
this is an incredibly brief presentation
on a very, very complex project.

212
00:10:51.360 --> 00:10:52.800
I've had to be very schematic.

213
00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:56.560
I hope that was enough to at least
give you a guide and some interest.

